page("beef-fajitas-skirt-steak","Beef Fajitas With Skirt Steak Over Mesquite","Tex-Mex beef fajitas on a small folding charcoal grill: marinated skirt steak seared hot over mesquite, with peppers and onions on the griddle.","grid2",recipe(
"<p>Fajitas started as a ranch-hand cut on the Texas border, and they're made for a small grill: skirt steak is thin and cooks in minutes over hot coals. Run a <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle plate</a> on one half for the peppers and onions.</p>",
"4","30 minutes plus 1–8 hours marinating",["1½–2 lb outside or inside skirt steak","Marinade: juice of 2 limes, ¼ cup oil, 3 cloves garlic, 1 tsp cumin, 1 tsp chili powder, 1 tsp salt, ½ tsp pepper","2 bell peppers and 1 large onion, sliced","Flour or corn tortillas","Pico de gallo, guacamole, sour cream, lime wedges"],
["Marinate the steak in the fridge 1 to 8 hours. Lime gets mushy past that.","Light a full chimney of lump, with a mesquite chunk. Pour a full, hot bed under the grate half and a thinner layer under the griddle.","Cook peppers and onions on the oiled griddle until charred and tender.","Pat the steak dry. Sear over the hottest coals 2 to 4 minutes per side to at least 145°F (many prefer 130–135°F for medium-rare; 145°F is the USDA safe minimum).","Rest 5 minutes. Slice thin <b>across</b> the grain.","Warm tortillas on the grate 10 seconds a side. Build at the table."],
tips=["Skirt steak's grain runs across its width; cut long pieces into 5–6&quot; sections first, then slice across.","Discard used marinade."]),"recipes",[])

page("chicken-fajitas-on-a-griddle-plate","Chicken Fajitas on a Griddle Plate","Chicken fajitas cooked on a carbon steel griddle plate over charcoal, sized for a small folding grill.","grid1",recipe(
"<p>Chicken fajitas are easier on a griddle than on grates: sliced chicken can't fall through, and the plate sears every surface.</p>",
"4","35 minutes plus marinating",["1½ lb boneless chicken thighs or breasts, sliced into strips","Same lime-cumin marinade as beef fajitas","2 bell peppers, 1 onion, sliced","Tortillas and toppings"],
["Marinate chicken 30 minutes to 4 hours in the fridge.","Build a hot coal bed under the griddle.","Cook peppers and onions first; push to the cooler end.","Add chicken in a single layer. Cook, turning, until browned and 165°F inside, 6 to 10 minutes.","Toss with the vegetables and serve with warm tortillas."],
tips=["Use separate tongs for raw and cooked chicken."]),"recipes",[])

page("breakfast-tacos-on-the-griddle","Breakfast Tacos on the Griddle","Texas breakfast tacos on a folding grill's griddle plate: bacon or chorizo, eggs, potatoes and salsa in warm tortillas.","grid1",recipe(
"<p>In Texas, breakfast tacos are the morning at deer camp, the tailgate at 8 a.m. and the river launch. One griddle plate does it all.</p>",
"4","35 minutes",["8 flour tortillas","½ lb bacon or Mexican chorizo","2 cups diced potatoes, parboiled","6 eggs, beaten","Shredded cheese, salsa or pico"],
["Light a moderate fire under the griddle.","Cook bacon or chorizo until done (chorizo to 160°F). Set aside.","Fry potatoes in the drippings until crisp.","Scramble eggs on the cooler part of the plate until firm.","Warm tortillas on the grate. Fill and top with cheese and salsa."],
tips=["Chop bacon before cooking for easier tacos.","Wrap finished tacos in foil and keep them on the cool side."]),"recipes",[])

page("venison-backstrap-on-a-folding-grill","Venison Backstrap on a Folding Grill","Grilling deer backstrap at camp on a small charcoal grill: trim, season, sear fast and rest, with safe temperatures.","full10",recipe(
"<p>Backstrap (loin) is the best cut from a Texas whitetail and should be cooked fast and hot. Venison is very lean, so it dries out if overcooked.</p>",
"4","25 minutes",["1½–2 lb venison backstrap, silver skin removed","2 tbsp oil","Salt, coarse pepper, garlic","Optional: bacon to wrap"],
["Bring the backstrap out 20 minutes before cooking. Oil and season.","Build a hot two-zone fire.","Sear over the coals, turning every 2 minutes, until browned all over.","Move to the cool side and cook to 145°F minimum, then rest 3 minutes. (USDA recommends 160°F for ground venison.)","Slice into medallions."],
tips=["Field-dress and cool game quickly; keep it cold.","If you wrap in bacon, cook until the bacon is crisp and the venison reaches at least 145°F."],size="full"),"recipes",[("What temperature should venison steaks be cooked to?","At least 145°F with a 3-minute rest for whole cuts; 160°F for ground venison.")])

page("bacon-wrapped-jalapeno-poppers","Bacon-Wrapped Jalapeño Poppers on a Charcoal Grill","Cream cheese stuffed jalapeños wrapped in bacon, cooked on the cool side of a small folding grill until the bacon is crisp.","c28",recipe(
"<p>Every Texas tailgate and dove-season camp has these. Cook them indirect so the bacon renders and the cheese doesn't spill into the coals.</p>",
"6–8 (as a snack)","40 minutes",["12 jalapeños, halved lengthwise and seeded","8 oz cream cheese, softened, mixed with ½ cup shredded cheddar","12 slices thin bacon, halved","Toothpicks (soaked)"],
["Fill pepper halves with the cheese mix.","Wrap each with a half slice of bacon; secure with a toothpick.","Build a two-zone fire. Arrange poppers cheese-side up on the cool side.","Cook 25 to 35 minutes until the bacon is fully cooked and crisp.","Crisp briefly near the coals if needed. Let cool a few minutes; filling is very hot."],
tips=["Wear gloves when seeding jalapeños.","A foil pan or perforated grill tray keeps drips off the coals."]),"recipes",[])

page("redfish-on-the-half-shell","Redfish on the Half Shell","A Texas Gulf Coast classic: redfish fillets cooked scales-on over charcoal on a small folding grill.","c08",recipe(
"<p>On the Texas coast, redfish is grilled \"on the half shell\": fillets left with the skin and scales on, cooked scale-side down so the fish steams in its own armor. No flipping, no sticking. Follow Texas fishing regulations for size and bag limits.</p>",
"4","30 minutes",["2 redfish fillets, skin and scales on (about 1 lb each)","3 tbsp melted butter with 2 cloves garlic","Salt, pepper, paprika or Cajun seasoning","Lemons"],
["Build a medium two-zone fire.","Brush the flesh side with garlic butter and season.","Lay fillets scale-side down over medium heat.","Cook without flipping 12 to 20 minutes, basting occasionally, until the flesh is opaque, flakes easily and reaches 145°F.","Lift off with a wide spatula. Spoon the meat off the skin and serve with lemon."],
tips=["Ask your fish cleaner to leave scales on.","Works with other firm white fillets too."]),"recipes",[])

page("grilled-street-corn","Grilled Street Corn (Elote)","Charred corn on the cob with mayo, cotija, chile and lime: a Tex-Mex side made on a small charcoal grill.","c28",recipe(
"<p>Corn is cheap, travels well and chars beautifully over the edge of the coals.</p>",
"6","25 minutes",["6 ears corn, husked","¼ cup mayonnaise or crema","½ cup crumbled cotija","Chili powder or Tajín","Lime wedges, cilantro"],
["Grill corn over medium coals, turning every couple of minutes, until charred in spots and tender, 10 to 15 minutes.","Brush with mayo or crema.","Roll in cotija; sprinkle chili powder.","Serve with lime."],
tips=["Keep mayo and cotija cold until use."]),"recipes",[])

page("texas-ribeye-over-mesquite","Texas Ribeye Over Mesquite Coals","A thick ribeye seasoned with salt and pepper, seared over mesquite coals on a folding grill and finished on the cool side.","full01",recipe(
"<p>Mesquite coals burn hot and give beef a bold, desert-campfire flavor. A thick ribeye on a two-zone fire is the best showcase.</p>",
"2","30 minutes",["2 ribeyes, 1–1½&quot; thick","Texas salt and pepper rub","Butter (optional)"],
["Season 45 minutes ahead or right before cooking.","Light mesquite lump; build a hot two-zone fire.","Sear over the hottest coals 2 to 3 minutes per side.","Move to the cool side; turn every minute or two until 5°F below your target. USDA safe minimum is 145°F plus a 3-minute rest.","Rest 5 minutes. Optional pat of butter on top."],
tips=["Render the fat edge against the grate with tongs.","Sear and slide keeps the outside from burning."],size="full"),"recipes",[])
